  - Its 2027 capital spending plan is up 18%.\n  - It is cutting 2027 transformer orders by about 40%. It is also resizing its Q1 2027 transformer tender, which was about C$900M, and deferring two substation programmes \"until financing improves.\"\n  - Pellham's own response shows it can't count on equity to fund the plan. It is cutting spending rather than waiting for the market.\n- **Norvane Utilities:**\n  - It plans 2027 spending up 12%, with a C$450M tender in Q2.\n  - I found no evidence that Norvane has tried to issue equity.\n  - The tracker says two utility offerings were cut back in Q3, but I could only identify Pellham's. I don't know who the second issuer was or how its offering was resized.\n- **Debt as the alternative:** n5's result says debt markets are open, but only for the stronger issuers. It says Norvane can fund itself that way. I didn't check the bond spread data myself.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- The evidence covers Q3 and October 2026 only. I didn't find share-price, valuation or regulatory data. Equity access could reopen if sentiment improves, and Pellham's wording suggests the deferred substation work depends on that.\n- The sources don't give Pellham's capex plan as a dollar figure, so I can't say how large its funding gap is.\n\n**For VLTX:** the main risk is Pellham, because Pellham is a major customer. Its transformer order cut and tender resize hit VLTX directly, since VLTX holdings won 60% of the last tender round. Norvane's tender looks safer.","ttft_ms":3332,"type":"result","duration_ms":50209,"uuid":"6617f841-b883-4450-91ca-756979cafbc5","ttft_stream_ms":3043,"time_to_request_ms":140,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":15,"system_prompt":8,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":4,"input_attachments":24,"input_hooks":4,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":6,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":13,"init_frame_send":1,"engine_pickup":4,"loop_entry":9,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":3,"request_announcements":17,"